<h2>Flood insurance questions in Buda</h2>
<p>Because so much of Buda’s storm damage involves rain and runoff, the line between flood and water damage matters. A homeowners policy generally excludes flood, so a home without a National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) or private flood policy may have no coverage for water that rises from outside. NFIP policies typically carry a 30-day waiting period, so coverage purchased after a forecast usually will not help with that storm. You can learn more at <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">floodsmart.gov</a> and check flood zones on the <a href="https://msc.fema.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FEMA Flood Map Service Center</a>.</p>
<p>If you do have flood coverage, the claim follows its own rules and timelines. If you only have a homeowners policy, it is still worth checking whether part of the damage came from a covered cause, such as wind-driven rain through a damaged roof.</p>

<h2>Permits and repairs in Buda</h2>
<p>The City of Buda has its own permit office. Its requirements are separate from the City of San Marcos, so ask the City of Buda what it needs before roof replacement, structural work, or major interior rebuilds. Properties outside city limits in unincorporated Hays County are not subject to county residential building permits, but the county does regulate septic, development, and floodplain matters. Whether your insurer pays for code-required upgrades depends on your ordinance or law coverage. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">building codes guide</a> covers the basics.</p>

<h2>Working with a San Marcos public adjuster in Buda</h2>
